{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}User Guide – Servers & Operations{% endblock %}

{% block content %}
{% set section_title = 'Servers & Operations' %}
{{ include('user_guide/_breadcrumb.html.twig') }}

<div class="d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center mb-4">
    <h1 class="h3 mb-0"><i class="bi bi-server me-2"></i>Servers &amp; Operations</h1>
    <a href="{{ path('servers_index') }}" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-primary">
        <i class="bi bi-arrow-right-circle me-1"></i>Go to Servers
    </a>
</div>

<div class="row g-4">
<div class="col-lg-9">

<div class="card mb-4" id="servers-overview">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-server me-1"></i>Servers Overview</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>The <strong>Servers → Servers</strong> page provides a consolidated view of all external systems that DashDDI integrates with:</p>
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li><strong>DNS Servers</strong> (BIND) — configure SSH credentials and trigger zone file pushes</li>
            <li><strong>DHCP Servers</strong> (Kea) — configure SSH credentials and trigger config pushes</li>
            <li><strong>Aruba CX Switches</strong> — query port status and trigger port actions</li>
            <li><strong>ClearPass Servers</strong> — manage NAC server connections</li>
            <li><strong>Snipe-IT Servers</strong> — configure the asset management integration</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-2 mb-0">From this page you can add new server connections, edit credentials, and manually trigger push operations to any server.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="dns-servers">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-globe me-1"></i>DNS Servers</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI connects to BIND DNS servers via SSH to upload zone files and reload the service. Each DNS server entry stores:</p>
        <dl class="row mb-0">
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Name</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">A label for the server (e.g. <code>ns1</code>)</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">IP Address</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The IPv4 or IPv6 address of the server. An IP address is required (not a hostname) because secondary servers reference this value directly in their BIND <code>primaries</code> configuration — if a hostname were used, secondary servers could not pull zone transfers during a DNS outage.</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SSH Username</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">The account DashDDI connects as</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">SSH Public Key</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Auto-generated by DashDDI — copy it to <code>authorized_keys</code> on the server</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Zone Directory</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Remote path where zone files are written</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Reload Command</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Command to reload BIND after files are uploaded (e.g. <code>sudo rndc reload</code>)</dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-3 mb-0 text-muted small">See the <a href="{{ path('user_guide_integrations') }}#dns-integration">DNS integration setup guide</a> for the full server-side configuration steps.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="push-logs">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-journal-code me-1"></i>Push Logs</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>Every time DashDDI pushes configuration to an external server, the result is logged. View the history at <strong>Servers → Push Logs</strong>.</p>
        <p>Each log entry includes:</p>
        <ul class="mb-0">
            <li>Timestamp</li>
            <li>Target server</li>
            <li>Whether the push succeeded or failed</li>
            <li>Full output from the remote commands (expand a row to see details)</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="mt-2 mb-0">If a scheduled push fails, check the Push Logs for the error message before investigating the server-side configuration.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="activity-log">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-shield-check me-1"></i>Activity Log</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>The Activity Log records all create, update, soft-delete, restore, and hard-delete operations on managed entities, plus user logins. View it at <strong>Activity Log</strong> in the top navigation.</p>
        <p>Each entry shows:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Timestamp, action type, and the entity affected — child entities include parent context (e.g. <em>photo-dir @ example.com</em> for a DNS record, <em>eth0 @ server01</em> for an interface)</li>
            <li>Which user performed the action and from which IP address</li>
            <li>A link to the affected entity where applicable — hard-deleted entities are shown as plain text since the record no longer exists</li>
            <li>For updates: the exact fields that changed with old and new values (click the chevron to expand)</li>
            <li>For deletes: a snapshot of all field values at the time of deletion, so the record is readable even after it is gone</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Sensitive fields (SSH keys, passwords, API keys) are shown as <code>[redacted]</code> so you can see that a credential changed without exposing the value.</p>
        <p><strong>Retention</strong> is configured in <strong>Application Settings → Activity Log</strong>. The default is 90 days; leave it blank to keep entries indefinitely. A <em>Purge Activity Logs</em> task is available in the Scheduler.</p>
        <p class="mb-0"><strong>Remote syslog forwarding</strong> is also configured in Application Settings. When enabled, every activity log entry is forwarded asynchronously to your syslog server in RFC 5424 format, including field-level change details. Both UDP and TCP transports are supported; TCP connections are kept alive across messages by the background worker.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="worker-queue">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-stack me-1"></i>Worker Queue</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI uses an asynchronous message queue to process background jobs (push operations, ClearPass sync, Snipe-IT pulls, etc.) without blocking the web interface. View the queue status at <strong>Servers → Worker Queue</strong>.</p>
        <p>The queue has two tiers:</p>
        <dl class="row mb-0">
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Priority</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Fast jobs — typically individual push notifications triggered by data changes</dd>
            <dt class="col-sm-3">Bulk</dt>
            <dd class="col-sm-9">Long-running jobs — full DNS/DHCP pushes, backups, sync operations</dd>
        </dl>
        <p class="mt-3 mb-0">The Worker Queue page shows the current message count in each queue and any failed messages. Use the <strong>Purge Failed</strong> button to clear stuck jobs after resolving the underlying issue.</p>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="scheduler">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-clock-history me-1"></i>Scheduled Tasks</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI can automate recurring operations on a cron schedule. Manage them at <strong>Other Settings → Scheduler</strong>.</p>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table table-sm table-bordered mb-0">
                <thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>DNS Push</td><td>Generate and deploy zone files to all DNS servers</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>DHCP Push</td><td>Generate and deploy Kea config to all DHCP servers</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Database Backup</td><td>Create an encrypted backup of the database</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>ClearPass Sync</td><td>Push managed endpoints to ClearPass and pull auth logs</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Snipe-IT Pull</td><td>Fetch asset and MAC data from Snipe-IT</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Log Purge</td><td>Remove old push logs and expired DHCP leases</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Host Purge</td><td>Permanently delete soft-deleted hosts past their retention window</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-3 mb-0">Each task shows its next scheduled run time and a <strong>Run Now</strong> button for manual execution. Click a task to view its full execution output history.</p>
        <div class="alert alert-info mt-3 mb-0 py-2 small">
            <i class="bi bi-lightbulb me-1"></i>Cron expressions are evaluated in the timezone configured under <strong>Application Settings</strong>. Use standard 5-field cron syntax (e.g. <code>0 2 * * *</code> for daily at 2 AM).
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="card mb-4" id="letsencrypt">
    <div class="card-header fw-semibold"><i class="bi bi-shield-lock me-1"></i>Let's Encrypt Certificate</div>
    <div class="card-body">
        <p>DashDDI can obtain and automatically renew a trusted TLS certificate for itself using its own DNS-01 challenge API — no port 80 exposure or manual certificate management required.</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Prerequisites</h6>
        <ul>
            <li>The DashDDI server must be registered in DashDDI as a <strong>Host</strong> with an A or AAAA record linked to one of its interfaces.</li>
            <li>That record's domain must have at least one <strong>DNS View</strong> marked as <em>Public</em> so Let's Encrypt can reach the <code>_acme-challenge</code> TXT record.</li>
            <li>DNS must be pushed to a BIND server that is authoritative for the domain.</li>
            <li>A general-purpose API token with <code>api_hosts_index</code> and <code>api_hosts_token_generate</code> permissions is required to run the Ansible role.</li>
        </ul>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Ansible setup (recommended)</h6>
        <p>The <code>certbot-dns-dashddi</code> Ansible role (included at <code>certbot-dns-dashddi/ansible/</code>) automates the entire setup. Run it against the DashDDI host with <code>dashddi_deploy_hook</code> pointing at the deploy script bundled in the repo:</p>
        <pre class="bg-light p-2 rounded small mb-2">ansible-playbook -i inventory certbot-dns-dashddi/ansible/certbot.yml \
  -e dashddi_url=https://dashddi.example.com \
  -e dashddi_admin_token=&lt;token&gt; \
  -e dashddi_deploy_hook=/opt/dashddi/docker/certbot-deploy.sh</pre>
        <p class="mb-0">The role generates a host-scoped token, installs certbot and the <code>certbot-dns-dashddi</code> plugin, and configures a systemd timer that renews the certificate twice daily. After each renewal the deploy hook copies the new cert into Docker's <code>ssl_certs</code> volume and restarts nginx automatically.</p>

        <h6 class="mt-3">Manual setup</h6>
        <p>Without Ansible, install certbot and the plugin manually (see <code>certbot-dns-dashddi/README.md</code>), generate a host-scoped token from the DashDDI host's page, then request the certificate once:</p>
        <pre class="bg-light p-2 rounded small mb-2">dashddi-certbot --credentials /etc/letsencrypt/dashddi.ini \
  -- --deploy-hook /opt/dashddi/docker/certbot-deploy.sh</pre>
        <p>For automatic renewal, copy the deploy hook into certbot's drop-in directory so it runs on every renewal without any extra arguments:</p>
        <pre class="bg-light p-2 rounded small mb-0">cp /opt/dashddi/docker/certbot-deploy.sh /etc/letsencrypt/renewal-hooks/deploy/</pre>
    </div>
</div>

</div>{# /col-lg-9 #}

<div class="col-lg-3 d-none d-lg-block">
    <div class="card sticky-top" style="top:80px">
        <div class="card-header small fw-semibold">On this page</div>
        <div class="list-group list-group-flush small">
            <a href="#servers-overview" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Servers Overview</a>
            <a href="#dns-servers" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">DNS Servers</a>
            <a href="#push-logs" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Push Logs</a>
            <a href="#activity-log" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Activity Log</a>
            <a href="#worker-queue" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Worker Queue</a>
            <a href="#scheduler" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Scheduled Tasks</a>
            <a href="#letsencrypt" class="list-group-item list-group-item-action py-1">Let's Encrypt Certificate</a>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

</div>{# /row #}
{% endblock %}
